1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,460
The following program was produced before the December 26, 2004 earthquake and tsunami struck South Asia.

2
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:15,260
They're among the deadliest of natural disasters.

3
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:21,900
Vicious sea predators that have taken tens of thousands of lives in the past century alone.

4
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:27,880
They strike with lethal force, decimating everything in their way.

5
00:00:27,881 --> 00:00:32,820
Today, scientists are beginning to understand these killer waves.

6
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:37,520
Computerized guoys, wave machines, satellites, and 3D models

7
00:00:37,521 --> 00:00:41,440
are helping to predict when and where the next deadly tsunami will hit.

8
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:44,280
It's a technological race against nature.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,080
Nature Tech Tsunamis, next on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:03,220
At the bottom of the sea lurks a beast like no other.

11
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:09,860
Tsunamis, the Japanese word for harbor waves, appear without warning.

12
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:14,920
They are triggered by seismic events, such as earthquakes and volcanoes,

13
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:19,920
that generate a displacement of the seafloor and massive underwater waves.

14
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:25,700
These waves travel undetected across the ocean at jet speed.

15
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:32,080
When they reach land, the waves slow down, but not before they've grown to terrifying

16
00:01:32,081 --> 00:01:38,880
heights, towering walls of water that can devour entire cities.

17
00:01:38,881 --> 00:01:42,540
Tsunamis are among the most misunderstood of all natural disasters.

18
00:01:42,541 --> 00:01:46,860
Unlike many other natural disasters, like hurricanes, you know that something's coming

19
00:01:46,861 --> 00:01:51,260
because the wind starts to blow really fast or it starts to rain really hard.

20
00:01:51,261 --> 00:01:55,440
There are no real natural signs in advance that a tsunami's coming until the water actually

21
00:01:55,441 --> 00:01:56,840
starts to move.

22
00:01:56,841 --> 00:02:03,840
For centuries, tsunamis have puzzled scientists because few people who have

23
00:02:03,841 --> 00:02:08,185
actually witnessed the disaster have lived to tell about it.

24
00:02:08,186 --> 00:02:10,140
But today, state-of-the-art

25
00:02:10,141 --> 00:02:18,006
technology is beginning to solve the mysteries behind this natural-born killer.

26
00:02:18,007 --> 00:02:19,300
It's a frigid

27
00:02:19,301 --> 00:02:21,202
afternoon in the North Pacific.

28
00:02:21,203 --> 00:02:24,760
These scientists are braving the stormy seas to deploy the

29
00:02:24,761 --> 00:02:29,400
most promising technology in the field of tsunami research.

30
00:02:29,401 --> 00:02:31,760
DART, or Deep Ocean Assessment

31
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:39,560
and Reporting of Tsunamis, are buoys that detect and measure a tsunami in the open ocean.

32
00:02:39,561 --> 00:02:46,560
The high-tech tool was engineered by NOAA, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Association.

33
00:02:46,561 --> 00:02:51,714
In a sense, it's a system of systems on guard looking for tsunamis.

34
00:02:51,715 --> 00:02:53,560
If something is reported

35
00:02:53,561 --> 00:02:58,760
reported out in the ocean, it's sent back to shore, where someone on shore can make a decision about,

36
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:01,520
do I call a tsunami warning, or is everything all normal?

37
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:05,437
This is how DART works.

38
00:03:05,438 --> 00:03:10,440
It consists of a seafloor bottom pressure recording system, or BPR,

39
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:17,640
capable of detecting tsunamis as small as one centimeter, and a moored surface buoy for real-time

40
00:03:17,641 --> 00:03:18,776
communications.

41
00:03:18,777 --> 00:03:24,080
An acoustic link is used to transmit data from the BPR on the seafloor

42
00:03:24,081 --> 00:03:25,486
to the surface buoy.

43
00:03:25,487 --> 00:03:30,760
The data is then relayed via a satellite link to ground stations throughout

44
00:03:30,761 --> 00:03:35,800
the Pacific, which disseminate information to tsunami warning centers.

45
00:03:35,801 --> 00:03:37,240
The goal is obvious,

46
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:43,580
to reduce loss of life and property. The interesting thing, it only takes about two

47
00:03:43,581 --> 00:03:49,300
minutes for this whole communication path to take place that goes from the seafloor to the buoy,

48
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:55,240
relay to the satellite to shore. Back at NOAA's headquarters in Seattle, Washington,

49
00:03:55,760 --> 00:04:01,820
a flagship for tsunami research, a team of scientists and engineers is constantly fine-tuning

50
00:04:01,821 --> 00:04:06,449
the mooring technology, which has taken 25 years to develop.

51
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:09,380
What we're looking here as part of the

52
00:04:09,381 --> 00:04:11,880
The DART system is the bottom pressure recorder.

53
00:04:11,881 --> 00:04:14,380
Now this is the part that's on the sea floor,

54
00:04:14,381 --> 00:04:17,380
and it's really the heart and soul of the entire system.

55
00:04:17,381 --> 00:04:21,380
Inside of electronics is a Bordon pressure tube sensor

56
00:04:21,381 --> 00:04:24,380
which can very, very accurately measure the pressure.

57
00:04:24,381 --> 00:04:27,380
And this is what's actually measuring the height of the water

58
00:04:27,381 --> 00:04:29,380
above the bottom pressure recorder.

59
00:04:29,381 --> 00:04:33,380
Inside the single board computer in the CPU is an acoustic modem

60
00:04:33,381 --> 00:04:35,380
that talks to this transducer out here.

61
00:04:35,381 --> 00:04:38,380
Now this is the unit that actually communicates with the buoy,

62
00:04:38,381 --> 00:04:40,280
and there's a similar unit on top of the buoy.

63
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:42,180
And these guys talk every hour,

64
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:44,960
make sure they're reading the proper pressure parameters

65
00:04:44,961 --> 00:04:48,320
and that everything's as normal in the normal condition.

66
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:50,300
We're always trying to improve the technology.

67
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:55,520
Presently what you have is a system that's reporting from the seafloor to a computer lab.

68
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:58,720
What we're hoping to do is make it a bi-directional system.

69
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:02,280
That is, the warning guidance guy or the researcher

70
00:05:02,281 --> 00:05:07,800
could sit behind the computer and actually talk to the seafloor and query it.

71
00:05:07,801 --> 00:05:09,860
say, I'd like to know what your pressure is right now.

72
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:13,220
And it'll report its pressures back in near real time.

73
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:17,220
For the last three years,

74
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,300
six buoys have been gathering data in earthquake-prone regions of the Pacific,

75
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:24,260
where most tsunamis have been triggered in the past.

76
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,060
But the DART system cannot work alone.

77
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:33,060
It relies on technology from the earthquake community

78
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:36,740
to answer the most fundamental technological questions.

79
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:39,600
What generates a tsunami?

80
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:41,880
How fast does it travel?

81
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:44,940
How large are its waves?

82
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:50,280
And most important, how much destruction will it cause once it hits land?

83
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:55,980
Tsunamis are often confused with tidal waves,

84
00:05:56,280 --> 00:06:00,540
which occur twice a day and are caused by the gravitational effects of the sun and moon.

85
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:05,340
Tsunamis, on the other hand, are rare,

86
00:06:05,341 --> 00:06:10,540
because a unique and infrequent series of circumstances is required to generate them.

87
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:15,540
Each year, approximately one or two tsunamis take place worldwide.

88
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:19,800
They are triggered after one or more violent geological events.

89
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:25,080
They can be caused by landslides that flow into the ocean and displace water,

90
00:06:25,081 --> 00:06:29,060
or submarine landslides that occur entirely undersea.

91
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:35,280
The explosion or collapse of underwater volcanoes can also trigger tsunamis.

92
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:42,460
But the largest and most destructive tsunamis are spawned by underwater earthquakes.

93
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:48,120
Large vertical shifts of the Earth's crust are caused by plates that rub together.

94
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:52,140
The plate boundaries where these shifts occur are called faults.

95
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:59,700
Throughout the Pacific Ocean, plates slip in a process known as subduction, which causes earthquakes to occur.

96
00:06:59,701 --> 00:07:03,900
the amount of vertical and horizontal motion of the sea floor

97
00:07:03,901 --> 00:07:09,120
may bring about a simultaneous slumping of underwater sediments due to the shaking.

98
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:15,940
The energy is transferred from the Earth's crust to the ocean water generating the tsunami.

99
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:21,960
If the sea floor were uplifted by a few meters over a very broad area,

100
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,620
you could imagine that it would actually create a mound of water on the ocean surface

101
00:07:27,621 --> 00:07:29,520
that would be a few meters high as well.

102
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:32,700
and this water would want to come back down,

103
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:34,760
but in the process of going back to equilibrium,

104
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:37,320
it's going to send out waves in all directions.

105
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:40,640
It's quite analogous to throwing a pebble in a pond

106
00:07:40,641 --> 00:07:43,560
and seeing the waves go out in all directions.

107
00:07:45,860 --> 00:07:47,880
Once the seismic event occurs,

108
00:07:47,881 --> 00:07:50,700
a tsunami begins to travel undersea.

109
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:56,340
Seismographs located in facilities around the world record its magnitude.

110
00:07:56,341 --> 00:08:01,340
The information is processed by sophisticated computers,

111
00:08:01,341 --> 00:08:05,340
which determine the height and wavelength of the tsunami.

112
00:08:05,341 --> 00:08:12,340
The wavelength is set by the size of the ocean bottom region that moves and generates the wave or waves.

113
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:19,340
Whereas waves caused by the wind are rarely longer than 1,000 feet from crest to crest,

114
00:08:19,341 --> 00:08:23,340
a tsunami wavelength can exceed 100 miles.

115
00:08:23,341 --> 00:08:30,340
miles. How fast a tsunami travels depends on the water depth and the acceleration of

116
00:08:30,341 --> 00:08:31,041
gravity.

117
00:08:31,042 --> 00:08:37,520
For example, where the ocean is approximately 18,000 feet deep, an unnoticed tsunami can

118
00:08:37,521 --> 00:08:45,135
move at 500 miles per hour and span the entire Pacific Ocean in just one day.

119
00:08:45,136 --> 00:08:46,520
This map shows

120
00:08:46,521 --> 00:08:50,966
the tsunami travel times for an earthquake off the coast of Chile.

121
00:08:50,967 --> 00:08:52,380
Each concentric curve

122
00:08:52,381 --> 00:08:55,260
represents one hour.

123
00:08:55,261 --> 00:08:58,560
In the open ocean, the tsunami may be hardly noticeable,

124
00:08:58,561 --> 00:09:01,380
perhaps only a few inches in height.

125
00:09:01,381 --> 00:09:06,260
As it travels into shallower water, the tsunami builds to a steep pitch,

126
00:09:06,261 --> 00:09:10,220
sometimes reaching a height of 30 to 70 feet.

127
00:09:10,221 --> 00:09:12,420
As it approaches the coastline, though,

128
00:09:12,421 --> 00:09:14,220
the water becomes much shallower.

129
00:09:14,221 --> 00:09:18,120
The energy is then confined to a smaller volume of water.

130
00:09:18,121 --> 00:09:20,080
and then the energy starts

131
00:09:20,081 --> 00:09:24,080
push the wave higher and higher.

132
00:09:24,081 --> 00:09:26,520
When a tsunami finally reaches the coast

133
00:09:26,521 --> 00:09:30,200
it may appear as rapidly rising or falling tides

134
00:09:30,201 --> 00:09:32,800
or a series of breaking waves.

135
00:09:32,801 --> 00:09:34,140
In the worst case,

136
00:09:34,141 --> 00:09:40,220
a tsunami comes on shore as a turbulent wall of water.

137
00:09:40,221 --> 00:09:45,960
One of the largest tsunamis in history struck Alaska's Lituya Bay in 1958.

138
00:09:45,961 --> 00:09:49,300
the water rushed up 1,740 feet,

139
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:52,860
almost 500 feet higher than the Empire State Building.

140
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:56,020
You can define tsunamis in two categories.

141
00:09:56,180 --> 00:09:59,540
One being locally generated, that's immediately off your coast,

142
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:02,880
and the tsunami arrives almost instantaneously.

143
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:08,780
And then distant tsunamis, where an earthquake may occur in Alaska, for example.

144
00:10:09,340 --> 00:10:12,300
It'll be local to Alaska people, but it may be a distant tsunami

145
00:10:12,301 --> 00:10:15,920
to people who live in Hawaii and the West Coast of the United States.

146
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:24,760
The last large Pacific-wide tsunami was triggered by an 8.6 magnitude earthquake off the coast

147
00:10:24,761 --> 00:10:27,360
of Chile in 1960.

148
00:10:27,361 --> 00:10:33,840
It killed over 2,000 people and caused a half a billion dollars in damage, not only in Chile,

149
00:10:33,841 --> 00:10:40,160
but as far away as Hawaii and Japan.

150
00:10:40,161 --> 00:10:43,640
A tsunami topples everything in its path.

151
00:10:43,641 --> 00:10:49,740
When it hits land, it strips beaches, undermines trees and floods hundreds of meters inland,

152
00:10:49,741 --> 00:10:53,300
crushing homes and taking human lives.

153
00:10:53,301 --> 00:11:00,920
To help mitigate future disasters, oceanographers and engineers at NOAA have an arsenal of technology.

154
00:11:00,921 --> 00:11:08,200
This includes computerized inundation models that map the danger zones on coastlines.

155
00:11:08,201 --> 00:11:15,700
simulates the probable tsunami for this particular area and we can, the model can then estimate

156
00:11:15,701 --> 00:11:18,860
the wave heights and the inundation distances.

157
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:25,180
The numerical models are made up of a set of mathematical equations, which are fed into

158
00:11:25,181 --> 00:11:26,677
high-speed computers.

159
00:11:26,678 --> 00:11:31,240
The models take into account the magnitude of the seismic event,

160
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:37,160
the topography of the ocean, and the makeup of the coastline where a tsunami is likely to hit.

161
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:41,920
The computer models also calculate the raw wave data

162
00:11:41,921 --> 00:11:44,540
signaled from the dark buoy systems out at sea.

163
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:49,540
So then the forecasters can determine how many waves there are,

164
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:51,700
what's the time spacing between the waves,

165
00:11:52,220 --> 00:11:54,420
what's the amplitude of the waves,

166
00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:57,480
and then this can in turn be fed into a model

167
00:11:57,481 --> 00:11:59,640
that can then give a little more guidance

168
00:11:59,641 --> 00:12:01,220
in terms of who you would evacuate,

169
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:03,740
what could be the expected arrival time,

170
00:12:03,741 --> 00:12:07,760
and what could be the amplitude that we might expect when it hits shore.

171
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:13,360
Today's technology has opened a window into the world of tsunamis.

172
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:16,580
But even with all of the latest high-tech tools,

173
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:22,740
scientists still rely on the past to get real answers about these giant killer waves.

174
00:12:24,140 --> 00:12:29,100
The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami was the deadliest in recorded history.

175
00:12:29,180 --> 00:12:35,680
The previous record was the 1703 tsunami at Awa, Japan, that killed more than 100,000 people.

176
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:38,360
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

177
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:46,620
Despite incredible advances in technology, we are still at the mercy of nature.

178
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:50,660
Seismologists cannot predict when earthquakes will occur,

179
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:54,020
and therefore cannot determine when tsunamis will be triggered.

180
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:58,820
As a result, scientists study historical tsunamis

181
00:12:58,821 --> 00:13:03,060
to better understand how to design instruments to predict future disasters.

182
00:13:04,060 --> 00:13:09,940
According to myth, a tsunami may explain the biblical story of Moses and the parting of the Red Sea

183
00:13:09,941 --> 00:13:12,500
when the Israelites were fleeing Egypt.

184
00:13:13,420 --> 00:13:19,220
As a tsunami reaches shore, water is sometimes withdrawn to expose the sea floor.

185
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:23,340
In this case, perhaps water was withdrawn from shore to shore,

186
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,920
which would have allowed the Israelites to cross.

187
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:34,740
however one of the first written records of a tsunami dates back to 1470 BC when the Greek

188
00:13:34,741 --> 00:13:43,020
philosopher Plato wrote a great civilization was destroyed by earthquakes and floods it is believed

189
00:13:43,021 --> 00:13:49,200
that he was referring to the volcano Santorini now on the Greek island of Thera which had erupted

190
00:13:49,201 --> 00:13:53,260
and caused catastrophic tsunami waves almost 300 feet high.

191
00:13:54,800 --> 00:13:57,000
They impacted the entire region.

192
00:13:57,960 --> 00:14:03,120
Some scientists speculate that this tsunami sunk the Lost Island civilization of Atlantis.

193
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:11,200
The Great Lisbon earthquake of 1755 generated a tsunami wave

194
00:14:11,201 --> 00:14:13,540
that penetrated a half mile into the city,

195
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:18,500
wiping out buildings and bridges and taking 50,000 lives.

196
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:26,200
In 1883, in the Sunda Straits between Java and Sumatra, now Indonesia,

197
00:14:26,201 --> 00:14:33,080
a volcano Krakatoa erupted and created tsunami waves as tall as a 12-story building.

198
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,820
It was the most catastrophic disaster in history at that time.

199
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:42,960
The waves swept over four miles inland, killing 33,000.

200
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:47,920
The world quickly learned about the disaster thanks to a new invention,

201
00:14:47,921 --> 00:14:49,500
the telegraph.

202
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:52,140
Just to give you an idea of the impact of the wave,

203
00:14:52,540 --> 00:14:56,820
it set up the oceans of the world in motion for seven days.

204
00:14:57,600 --> 00:14:59,820
The tsunami was being recorded in England

205
00:14:59,821 --> 00:15:03,340
on the other side of the world for seven days later.

206
00:15:03,860 --> 00:15:08,060
It was really the largest tsunami we know of in history.

207
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:13,460
Although tsunami-like disasters have been documented throughout time,

208
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:15,840
it wasn't until the 20th century

209
00:15:15,841 --> 00:15:17,880
that scientists began to make a connection

210
00:15:17,881 --> 00:15:21,540
between seismic events and tsunami wave formations.

211
00:15:22,660 --> 00:15:26,320
They used the Richter scale to measure earthquake magnitudes

212
00:15:26,321 --> 00:15:30,240
and tide gauges to monitor tide levels in harbors.

213
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:34,960
A tide gauge is used in most ports and harbors in the U.S.

214
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:37,480
to determine when high and low tides are

215
00:15:37,481 --> 00:15:40,920
because many ships that come into channels now,

216
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:43,160
they have such deep drafts

217
00:15:43,161 --> 00:15:45,760
that sometimes they can only come in and out at high tides.

218
00:15:45,840 --> 00:15:51,480
but they also can serve as tsunami gauges, but not very accurate ones.

219
00:15:53,680 --> 00:15:58,340
Traditional tide gauges only provide data after a tsunami has arrived.

220
00:15:59,960 --> 00:16:05,440
This delay can cost lives in tsunami-prone regions of the world, such as the Hawaiian Islands.

221
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:12,720
Hawaii sits in the middle of the ocean, which makes it especially vulnerable to Pacific-wide tsunamis.

222
00:16:12,721 --> 00:16:19,720
The islands also are volcanically active, which can generate local tsunamis.

223
00:16:19,721 --> 00:16:30,720
The basic technology that existed in the 1940s was not sophisticated enough to warn the public of a Pacific-wide tsunami that was bearing down on the Hawaiian Islands.

224
00:16:30,721 --> 00:16:40,860
On April 1st, 1946, in the early morning hours, a large earthquake erupted near the Aleutian

225
00:16:40,861 --> 00:16:44,651
Islands in Alaska and produced a large tsunami.

226
00:16:44,652 --> 00:16:48,120
Four and a half hours later, eight colossal

227
00:16:48,121 --> 00:16:51,480
sea waves pummeled the small town of Hilo, Hawaii.

228
00:16:51,481 --> 00:16:57,440
The large tsunami that came from the Aleutian Islands and struck on April 1st, 1946, had

229
00:16:57,441 --> 00:17:01,160
the unfortunate coincidence of occurring on April Fool's Day.

230
00:17:01,161 --> 00:17:03,300
And since there was no official warning,

231
00:17:03,301 --> 00:17:05,420
when people saw the waves and yelled,

232
00:17:05,421 --> 00:17:07,880
tidal wave, the term they used at the time,

233
00:17:07,881 --> 00:17:14,300
many people responded with, April Fool, thought it was a joke.

234
00:17:14,301 --> 00:17:17,240
Many residents working or living near the coastline

235
00:17:17,241 --> 00:17:19,760
couldn't outrun the massive mounds of water

236
00:17:19,761 --> 00:17:23,080
