And if the next tsunami is a few feet higher, it comes right over the top of these walls.

464
00:35:34,821 --> 00:35:40,820
Now we have a wonderful wall and have evacuation routes.

465
00:35:40,821 --> 00:35:45,820
But what if we have a bigger tsunami that could go over the wall?

466
00:35:45,821 --> 00:35:48,820
Truly, I still have concerns.

467
00:35:48,821 --> 00:36:00,800
To fine-tune their technology, Japanese scientists are soliciting help from an international community of tsunami experts.

468
00:36:01,480 --> 00:36:06,560
And much of the cutting-edge research is being conducted on the other side of the Pacific.

469
00:36:10,340 --> 00:36:12,920
California is earthquake country.

470
00:36:12,921 --> 00:36:20,340
And where there are earthquakes, there is the risk of tsunamis.

471
00:36:22,440 --> 00:36:25,600
At the University of Southern California in Los Angeles,

472
00:36:26,280 --> 00:36:28,360
scientists aren't afraid to get their hands wet

473
00:36:28,361 --> 00:36:32,100
to better understand this unpredictable and deadly phenomenon.

474
00:36:33,280 --> 00:36:37,100
They've built a shake table which simulates what happens to structures

475
00:36:37,101 --> 00:36:38,660
when a tsunami strikes them.

476
00:36:38,661 --> 00:36:40,060
They will spill most of the water.

477
00:36:40,620 --> 00:36:46,060
Kostas Sinalakis, a professor of civil engineering, heads up this cutting-edge technology.

478
00:36:47,200 --> 00:36:54,280
A shake table is a piece of equipment that is used to reproduce in the laboratory at a small scale

479
00:36:54,281 --> 00:36:59,280
the kind of vibration that we're likely to encounter during a big earthquake.

480
00:36:59,920 --> 00:37:03,260
So whenever we want to test a building component, we shake it.

481
00:37:03,261 --> 00:37:07,780
And we try to understand the wave patterns that form.

482
00:37:08,660 --> 00:37:13,700
The shake table is controlled by hydraulic cylinders that move the table sideways

483
00:37:13,701 --> 00:37:18,280
and recreate how tsunami waves respond in various environments.

484
00:37:19,480 --> 00:37:23,160
Here, engineers are testing the effect of a tsunami on a reservoir.

485
00:37:24,780 --> 00:37:27,680
This is a scale model of a real reservoir.

486
00:37:28,180 --> 00:37:33,280
And what we will do is we're actually going to generate a small tsunami inside this reservoir.

487
00:37:34,280 --> 00:37:37,460
And we're going to do it in the same way that the tsunami generated in nature,

488
00:37:37,461 --> 00:37:43,120
by actually shaking the seafloor, in the case of the ocean, or the bottom of a reservoir.

489
00:37:43,740 --> 00:37:47,380
So what we're worried in a reservoir is that during the earthquake,

490
00:37:48,020 --> 00:37:53,620
the reservoir does not get into vibration at its natural frequency,

491
00:37:54,040 --> 00:37:55,760
because that can be catastrophic.

492
00:37:57,400 --> 00:38:03,780
The 1994 Northridge, California earthquake triggered 20-foot-high waves in the Los Angeles reservoir.

493
00:38:03,781 --> 00:38:08,180
The water spilled over the dam and posed a threat to the city.

494
00:38:09,240 --> 00:38:14,840
In this case, an inland earthquake shook this reservoir, which in turn caused flooding.

495
00:38:15,600 --> 00:38:20,860
However, engineers used this incident to test the real dangers of an offshore earthquake,

496
00:38:21,120 --> 00:38:25,460
which could trigger tsunami waves that would inundate coastal communities.

497
00:38:27,180 --> 00:38:28,300
No, no, there's a bluff here.

498
00:38:29,080 --> 00:38:33,220
Costas Sinalakis and his colleagues are true tsunami chasers.

499
00:38:33,221 --> 00:38:40,440
These scientists have traveled the world to investigate the aftermath of some of the worst catastrophes in recent history.

500
00:38:42,300 --> 00:38:50,540
Their task force surveyed the 1992 tsunami which struck Flores Island, Indonesia, and killed approximately 1,700.

501
00:38:51,960 --> 00:38:58,400
In 1998, they inspected the Papua New Guinea disaster where approximately 2,000 perished.

502
00:38:58,401 --> 00:39:08,840
When we go to a devastated area, our main objective is just like sleuths in a detective novel.

503
00:39:09,340 --> 00:39:11,140
We're trying to piece together what happened.

504
00:39:11,560 --> 00:39:12,980
What direction did the wave come in?

505
00:39:13,380 --> 00:39:14,740
How many waves were there?

506
00:39:15,360 --> 00:39:19,700
If we had better technology, if you will, in better houses, a better warning system,

507
00:39:20,180 --> 00:39:22,340
would that have helped them survive?

508
00:39:22,341 --> 00:39:29,340
Their field work includes the use of video cameras and global positioning systems, or GPS,

509
00:39:29,341 --> 00:39:34,340
to map the devastated region and study the impact of the tsunami.

510
00:39:34,341 --> 00:39:39,340
The most high-tech piece of equipment that we use out in the field is a hand-held GPS,

511
00:39:39,341 --> 00:39:43,340
and we use that just to record our location,

512
00:39:43,341 --> 00:39:48,340
and then the GPS, we're able to download the exact coordinates into a computer,

513
00:39:48,341 --> 00:39:50,340
and we can make more detailed maps.

514
00:39:50,341 --> 00:40:00,500
Back at their tsunami research laboratory, the disaster detectives use this data to better understand similar seismic sea waves in their own backyard.

515
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:11,700
Here at USC, we're focusing primarily on the nearshore tsunami sources from earthquakes and landslides, things that exist very close to the Southern California coast.

516
00:40:12,660 --> 00:40:18,200
Offshore of Southern California, there's not only earthquake sources of tsunamis, but there's potential landslide sources of tsunamis.

517
00:40:18,201 --> 00:40:22,800
This animation shows a landslide tsunami being generated offshore of Palos Verdes.

518
00:40:23,120 --> 00:40:26,960
The initial generation area of the wave is smaller than the earthquake source,

519
00:40:27,220 --> 00:40:31,700
but the wave amplitude is larger, and the wave propagation is similar

520
00:40:31,701 --> 00:40:35,680
in that the wave heights are focused and amplified into the harbor areas

521
00:40:35,681 --> 00:40:37,620
and along certain sections of the coast.

522
00:40:39,520 --> 00:40:45,160
California has experienced, on average, one to two major tsunamis each century.

523
00:40:45,161 --> 00:40:52,160
In May of 2003, the scientists at USC made headlines, warning about the dangers of tsunamis.

524
00:40:52,161 --> 00:41:02,160
Their data predicts that a magnitude 6.5 or greater earthquake or underwater landslide off the coast of Southern California

525
00:41:02,161 --> 00:41:08,160
could produce eight-foot waves in the ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach.

526
00:41:08,161 --> 00:41:19,400
I've been trying for over 20 years to convince seismologists and others that there is a local tsunami hazard in Southern California.

527
00:41:20,160 --> 00:41:26,320
Only the past 10 years, maybe less, have people started to take it seriously.

528
00:41:27,020 --> 00:41:32,160
Just like on land, we have a lot of mountains and valleys that continues under the ocean here in Southern California.

529
00:41:32,161 --> 00:41:33,045
California.

530
00:41:33,046 --> 00:41:39,160
That is a result of faulting and earthquakes and when earthquakes and faults

531
00:41:39,161 --> 00:41:42,160
push the sea floor up or down we generate tsunamis.

532
00:41:42,161 --> 00:41:50,160
How great is a tsunami threat to Southern California? The question has spawned a new

533
00:41:50,161 --> 00:41:57,160
field of underwater paleoseismology which studies prehistoric earthquakes on the ocean floor

534
00:41:57,161 --> 00:42:00,640
and determines whether they have triggered tsunamis in the past.

535
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:08,060
Scientists are beginning to investigate this undersea frontier using submersibles,

536
00:42:09,020 --> 00:42:12,180
the kinds used to discover sunken ships like the Titanic.

537
00:42:14,580 --> 00:42:19,680
This video taken off the coast of Southern California reveals fault scarps,

538
00:42:20,600 --> 00:42:25,340
areas where the seafloor has literally been lifted up by prehistoric earthquakes.

539
00:42:25,341 --> 00:42:31,540
On the submersible, on Alvin, for example, there are sampling instruments.

540
00:42:32,080 --> 00:42:37,500
We have little tubes, maybe that long, about three feet long, a few inches in diameter,

541
00:42:37,900 --> 00:42:45,620
that we can push into the mud at the bottom to take samples to get an idea of how recent that earthquake may have been.

542
00:42:46,280 --> 00:42:50,060
We also obviously have cameras, video cameras, still cameras.

543
00:42:50,061 --> 00:42:56,340
We can use magnetometers, measure the magnetic field, which gives us an idea about the structure under the ocean.

544
00:42:56,620 --> 00:42:58,900
There's great hope because of all this new technology.

545
00:43:01,960 --> 00:43:05,800
As technology marches forward on land and undersea,

546
00:43:06,660 --> 00:43:11,640
scientists hope to one day have the proper tools to accurately predict tsunamis.

547
00:43:13,640 --> 00:43:19,520
Since tsunamis don't occur with regularity, it's difficult to get the proper funding to study them.

548
00:43:20,060 --> 00:43:27,100
But scientific data has proven that tsunamis are a real threat, therefore worth the research and money.

549
00:43:27,840 --> 00:43:34,000
At USC, scientists believe a major tsunami will strike the shores of Southern California in the near future.

550
00:43:34,420 --> 00:43:38,120
The question I'm often asked is when we're due for the next tsunami.

551
00:43:38,580 --> 00:43:46,440
I would say there is better than 50% chance that we're going to get a tsunami in the next 50 years.

552
00:43:46,940 --> 00:43:49,080
We will see one within our own lifetimes.

553
00:43:50,060 --> 00:43:55,360
I have sometimes the fantasy of actually being on the beach when the tsunami comes in.

554
00:43:55,560 --> 00:43:58,400
I've looked at hundreds of computer models of tsunamis.

555
00:43:58,560 --> 00:44:01,260
I mean, I've read a lot of papers on tsunamis.

556
00:44:01,760 --> 00:44:07,080
And yes, I am very curious to actually see one and in fact feel one,

557
00:44:07,300 --> 00:44:09,260
which is absolutely the wrong thing to do.

558
00:44:12,460 --> 00:44:14,400
I would love to see a tsunami happen.

559
00:44:14,401 --> 00:44:16,980
If you can be in a safe place on a high cliff,

560
00:44:16,981 --> 00:44:20,820
I would definitely like to see one going on.

561
00:44:21,820 --> 00:44:27,060
One thing technology has proven is that a tsunami will strike again.

562
00:44:28,040 --> 00:44:30,700
It's just a matter of when and where.

563
00:44:34,580 --> 00:44:40,120
To make a donation to support disaster relief in South Asia, go to historychannel.com.

